Worktree: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/182-dispatch-lint-held-fixes (your current directory).
Spec folder: specs/cli-external-orchestration/082-opencode-v1-v2-dual-compat/040-dispatch-lint-held-fixes (pre-approved, skip Gate 3)

ROLE: Act as a code agent. Make one change to how `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/pi/dispatch-preflight-lint.ts` reads quotes and command substitutions in the user's text, plus tests.

Problems: `use “cli-devin”` authorizes, because curly double quotes are not read as quotes. `$(echo $(true) cli-devin)` authorizes, because `isInsideExpansion` compares the last `$(` with the last `)`, so a closed inner substitution hides the still-open outer one.

Fix:
1. Quotes: where `isUsableMention` normalizes curly apostrophes, also normalize the curly and low double quotes `“`, `”`, `„`, `‟` to `"` and the curly and low single quotes `‘`, `’`, `‚`, `‛` to `'` (one character each, so indexes hold), before the in-word apostrophe handling and the quote check. So a curly-quoted mention is inside a quote, and a mixed pair (`“cli-devin"`) is too.
2. Substitutions: rewrite `isInsideExpansion` to scan the text before the index once, keeping a depth: `$(` opens one level, a `(` while depth > 0 opens one more, a `)` while depth > 0 closes one; a backtick toggles a separate open flag. The index is inside an expansion when depth > 0 or a backtick span is open. Update its comment.

Tests: in `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/pi/dispatch-preflight-lint.test.ts`, add to 'Pi refusal phrasings' (rows are [raw text, denied]):
denied (true):
  'use “cli-devin”'
  'use ‘cli-devin’'
  'say “run /deep:review --executor cli-devin”'
  'use “cli-devin"'
  'use "cli-devin”'
  'use „cli-devin“'
  '$(echo $(true) cli-devin)'
  '$(echo $(echo $(true)) cli-devin)'
  '$(a $(b) $(c) cli-devin)'
  '$(echo (1) cli-devin)'
allowed (false):
  '$(true) and then use cli-devin'
  'the “fast” path: use cli-devin'
Every other test must keep passing.

Comments keep the durable WHY and never name spec paths, phases, or task, review or finding ids.

